<!-- ============ CHAPTER 2 :: FLOYD + JAN 6 ============ -->
<section class="chapter" id="ch2" style="background: var(--paper-2);">
  <div class="ch-inner reveal">
    <span class="ch-tag blue">// CHAPTER TWO</span>
    <div class="ch-num">02</div>
    <h1 class="ch-title">PROTEST, POLITICS, &amp; THE CAPITOL</h1>
    <p class="ch-meme">when the news got even louder</p>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">On May 25, 2020, a Black man named <strong>George Floyd</strong> was murdered by a Minneapolis police officer named Derek Chauvin. Chauvin knelt on Floyd's neck during an arrest. A teenager named Darnella Frazier filmed it. The video spread everywhere.</p>

      <p>What happened next was huge. People in all fifty states and in dozens of countries marched. Some say the protests after Floyd's murder were among the biggest in U.S. history, with estimates between 15 million and 26 million people taking part.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="image-card">
      <img src="" data-img="floyd_protest" alt="A large crowd of masked protesters in New York City sits in the street holding up a painted portrait of George Floyd.">
      <div class="caption">// New York City, June 2020. people kneeling in silence. the portrait is George Floyd. notice the masks. they were still in the middle of a pandemic and they marched anyway.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>The protests were about Floyd, but not just about Floyd. They were about policing, racism, accountability, and the long history of unequal treatment of Black Americans. Most protests were peaceful. Some places saw property damage, fires, and clashes with police. The movement made cities, schools, companies, and families talk about things they had been avoiding.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="image-card">
      <div class="sensitive-wrap" data-sensitive>
        <div class="sensitive-cover">
          <span class="warning">⚠ INTENSE IMAGE</span>
          <div>A protester standing on top of a burned-out police car during demonstrations in Los Angeles.</div>
          <small>tap anywhere to reveal</small>
        </div>
        <img src="" data-img="burning_car" alt="A protester with their fist raised stands atop a burned police car in Los Angeles during May 2020 protests.">
      </div>
      <div class="caption">// Los Angeles, summer 2020. the same week saw quiet marches with candles and chaotic moments like this one. both happened in the same movement.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="meme-callout" style="border-left-color: var(--cyan); box-shadow: 6px 6px 0 var(--cyan);">
      "I CAN'T BREATHE"
      <small>// THREE WORDS THAT SHOWED UP ON SHIRTS, SIGNS, AND MURALS AROUND THE WORLD</small>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>That same year, <strong>Joe Biden</strong> beat Donald Trump in the 2020 presidential election. Biden won 306 <span class="vocab" data-term="electoral_college">Electoral College</span> votes to Trump's 232. That is the official, certified result.</p>

      <p>But Trump did not accept that he lost. He claimed the election had been stolen. Courts, state election officials, recounts, and his own federal agencies all looked into his claims. They did not find evidence to change the outcome. He kept claiming it anyway.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="image-card" style="max-width: 360px; margin: 32px auto;">
      <img src="" data-img="biden" alt="Joe Biden speaking at a podium.">
      <div class="caption">// Joe Biden, sworn in as the 46th President of the United States on January 20, 2021.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>On <strong>January 6, 2021</strong>, Congress met at the U.S. Capitol to certify the Electoral College results. That is a legal step the country has done for over 200 years. A normal day.</p>

      <p>This time, after a rally near the White House where Trump told supporters to march to the Capitol, a mob of his supporters attacked the building. They smashed windows. They beat police officers. They hunted lawmakers. People died. The constitutional <span class="vocab" data-term="transfer_of_power">transfer of power</span> was interrupted. Congress finished certifying the results that night anyway, hours behind schedule. Trump was later impeached by the House for a second time over the attack.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="image-card">
      <img src="" data-img="jan6" alt="A crowd of Trump supporters with Trump 2020 flags gathered at the U.S. Capitol building on January 6, 2021.">
      <div class="caption">// January 6, 2021. a mob attacked the U.S. Capitol while Congress was inside doing the constitutional step of confirming the next president. that had never happened before.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="vibe-box pink">
      <h4>VIBE CHECK :: 2020 had layers</h4>
      <p>Inside the same year: a pandemic, the biggest protests in U.S. history, a deeply contested presidential election, the Olympics getting postponed for the first time ever, a Mars rover named Perseverance launching, Tiger King, and TikTok hitting a billion users. Time felt like it stopped moving and also moved way too fast.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="reflect">
      <h4>REFLECT</h4>
      <p style="margin:0 0 8px;">Some questions to sit with:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>What is the difference between losing an election and saying you didn't lose? Why does that difference matter for a country?</li>
        <li>The text says democracy depends on accepting lawful results and the peaceful transfer of power. What do you think happens to a country if leaders stop doing that?</li>
        <li>Most of the 2020 protests were peaceful, but some weren't. How do you think a movement should be judged when it's that big and that many people are involved?</li>
      </ol>
      <textarea placeholder="if you want to write down what you think, here's the space..."></textarea>
    </div>

    <div class="quickcheck">
      <h4>chapter 2</h4>
