</section>

<!-- ============ CHAPTER 3 :: BREXIT / INFLATION / DOBBS ============ -->
<section class="chapter" id="ch3">
  <div class="ch-inner reveal">
    <span class="ch-tag green">// CHAPTER THREE</span>
    <div class="ch-num">03</div>
    <h1 class="ch-title">UNSETTLED SYSTEMS</h1>
    <p class="ch-meme">old fights came back wearing new clothes</p>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">The 2020s also inherited unfinished business. Big arguments that started years before came roaring back, sharper, more legal, and way more expensive.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>First up :: <strong>Brexit</strong>. That's a smush of "British" plus "exit." In 2016, British voters narrowly chose in a <span class="vocab" data-term="referendum">referendum</span> to leave the European Union. Supporters said Britain should get back control over its own laws, borders, and trade. Opponents warned it would hurt the economy, weaken Britain's influence, and complicate life in Northern Ireland. After years of arguing, the UK formally left the EU at 11 p.m. on January 31, 2020. The full economic separation finished at the end of that year.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="image-card">
      <img src="" data-img="brexit" alt="A man holds a red sign reading WE VOTED LEAVE next to a blurred European Union flag.">
      <div class="caption">// London. the EU flag in front, the leave-supporter sign behind. Brexit is one of those things people had strong feelings on even years after the vote.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>Next up :: <strong>inflation</strong>. That's a fancy word for prices going up across the board, which means your money buys less than it used to. After the pandemic, prices in the U.S. jumped hard. Why? A messy combination. Factories and ports got tangled up. Workers got sick or quit. Demand for stuff stayed strong. The government had given out a lot of pandemic money. Then Russia invaded Ukraine and energy and food got even more expensive.</p>

      <p>The numbers tell the story. From December 2020 to December 2021, U.S. consumer prices rose 7.0%. That was the biggest December-to-December jump since 1981. The cost of eggs, gas, used cars, and rent all became regular news topics.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="vibe-box green">
      <h4>VIBE CHECK :: "have you seen how much eggs are???"</h4>
      <p>Eggs basically became a personality trait in 2022 and 2023. People posted their receipts. Memes piled up about $7 dozen eggs. A bird flu outbreak made it worse. Eggs were never just about eggs. They were a fast, visible way to feel inflation in your gut every time you went to the store.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>Then in <strong>2022</strong>, the U.S. Supreme Court did something it almost never does. In a case called <strong>Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization</strong>, the Court overturned <span class="vocab" data-term="roe">Roe v. Wade</span>, which had been the law of the land since 1973. Roe had said the U.S. Constitution protected a right to abortion. Dobbs said no, it didn't. That sent the question of abortion law back to each individual state.</p>

      <p>The result was immediate. Some states banned or sharply restricted abortion. Other states moved to protect access. Supporters of Dobbs argued Roe had been wrongly decided in the first place and that abortion law should be decided democratically by states. Critics argued the Court removed a fundamental right and created very different rules depending on where you happen to live.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="meme-callout">
      "OLD ARGUMENTS DID NOT DISAPPEAR.<br>THEY CAME BACK SHARPER."
      <small>// THE PATTERN OF THE EARLY 2020s</small>
    </div>

    <div class="reflect">
      <h4>REFLECT</h4>
      <p style="margin:0 0 8px;">Some heavier thinking:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Inflation is a word about numbers, but it shows up as worry in real families. Has your family ever talked about something costing more than it used to? What was it?</li>
        <li>When something is decided "by states" instead of "by the whole country," your rights can change just because you cross a border. Is that fair? Why or why not?</li>
      </ol>
      <textarea placeholder="space to think on the page..."></textarea>
    </div>

    <div class="quickcheck">
      <h4>chapter 3</h4>
      <div class="q" data-qc='{"correct":2,"feedback":"correct. Brexit is the United Kingdom leaving the European Union. it finalized in 2020."}'>
