    <div class="meme-callout" style="border-left-color: var(--pink); box-shadow: 6px 6px 0 var(--pink);">
      "TWO STORIES, ONE WAR.<br>BOTH ARE FULL OF GRIEF."
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>The other big story of the 2020s is the rivalry between the <strong>United States and China</strong>. This is not exactly a new Cold War because the two countries trade with each other constantly and their economies are tangled together. But they compete over a lot: <span class="vocab" data-term="semiconductors">semiconductors</span>, artificial intelligence, military power in the Pacific Ocean, Taiwan, surveillance tech, human rights, and who gets to control the world's data. It is one of the central conflicts of the decade.</p>

      <p>And then there's <strong>migration</strong>. War, poverty, climate stress, gangs, dictators, and inequality pushed millions of people across borders. Governments argued about how to handle it. Many democratic countries saw their leaders lose elections because voters were unhappy about prices, immigration, or both. Trust in big institutions, in news, in government, in science, in tech companies, dropped almost everywhere. The 2020s became a decade where many people felt the system was too big to understand and too broken to trust.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="vibe-box pink">
      <h4>VIBE CHECK :: trust the algo, not the news</h4>
      <p>Polls in this decade kept showing the same thing. People said they trusted scientists less. They trusted journalists less. They trusted Congress, the Supreme Court, and the police less. They trusted strangers online less. And yet they kept scrolling. For a generation born into smartphones, "I don't trust anything I read" became a personality.</p>
    </div>

